{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F19AnInfiniteActualPassesAnyAssertion",
    "description": "REPRO WITH INVERTED POLARITY: this file PASSES today, and that is the defect. Every other repro in this directory asserts the behaviour we want and fails; this one asserts three MUTUALLY CONTRADICTORY values -- 42, -5 and 0 -- for one cell, and `esm test` reports 3 passed, 0 failed.\n\nThe cell's value is +inf: `big` sums 1e200 * 1e200 over an axis. An assertion whose ACTUAL value is non-finite is judged by `|actual - expected| <= rtol*|expected| + atol`, and with `actual = inf` that comparison is vacuously satisfied under the relative-tolerance arithmetic the runner uses, so `expected` stops mattering entirely. NaN does NOT do this -- a NaN actual is reported `actual=NaN ... FAIL`, correctly -- so the hole is specific to an infinity, which is exactly the value an overflow, a division by a zero denominator, or a `log(0)` produces.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE MORE THAN ELSEWHERE. This repository has no other gate: CLAUDE.md puts all model logic in `.esm` and all testing through `esm test`, and docs/esm-conventions.md \u00a713 turns that into a rule -- every fixture assertion names a value out of docs/nonroad-logging-county.md \u00a76 so that a source which silently delivered its `default` fails at the assertion rather than at the comparison. That discipline assumes an assertion cannot pass for a reason unrelated to its expected value. Six defects in this port have already returned a plausible wrong value from a document that validates; this one returns a PASS from a document that computes nothing meaningful at all.\n\nThe exposure is real and not hypothetical: an emission factor divided by a zero `medianLifeFullLoad`, a growth factor over a zero base indicator, or a Float32 overflow in a product of large sales figures each yield an infinity, and the assertion written to catch it would report `ok`.\n\n**Fix shape.** Judge finiteness before tolerance: an assertion whose actual value is not finite fails unless `expected` is the same infinity. One guard in the assertion comparison, alongside the NaN guard that is already there and already right.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "finding",
      "expected-to-pass-and-that-is-the-defect",
      "assertions",
      "silent"
    ]
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "probe_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 2
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "F19AnInfiniteActualPassesAnyAssertion": {
      "tolerance": {
        "rel": 1e-09
      },
      "variables": {
        "v": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "probe_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "1e200 in every cell -- finite, and asserted as such below."
        },
        "big": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "probe_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Sum of v[j]*v[j], which overflows to +inf."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "lhs": "v",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "makearray",
            "args": [],
            "regions": [
              [
                [
                  1,
                  1
                ]
              ],
              [
                [
                  2,
                  2
                ]
              ]
            ],
            "values": [
              1e+200,
              1e+200
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "big",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [
              "v"
            ],
            "output_idx": [
              "i"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "i": {
                "from": "probe_rows"
              },
              "j": {
                "from": "probe_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "*",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "v",
                    "j"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "v",
                    "j"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "an_infinite_actual_is_judged_against_expected",
          "description": "Three MUTUALLY CONTRADICTORY assertions on one cell: big[1] is +inf and cannot equal 42, -5 and 0 at once, yet today all three pass. The control is that the runner does compare finite values -- v[1] = 1e200 against 42 fails, and a NaN actual fails too; only an infinity slips through. When this file starts FAILING, the hole is closed.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "big",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 42.0,
              "coords": {
                "probe_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "big",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -5.0,
              "coords": {
                "probe_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "big",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "probe_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
